    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Liberation theology interprets the Gospel through the lens of the oppressed, and its best-known phrase, the preferential option for the poor, describes an obligation of those who call themselves Christian first of all to care for the poor and vulnerable.</li><li>It drew heavily on Marxist social analysis, though it did not adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> wholesale, and the Vatican's Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith, led by Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger, criticised it in the mid-1980s for excessive politicisation.</li><li>Its ideas spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, into black theology, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> theology, Palestinian liberation theology and Protestant thought, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a> later drew on liberationist ideas.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Hélder Câmara, the Brazilian archbishop, said: &quot;When I give food to the poor, they call me a saint. When I ask why they are poor, they call me a communist.&quot;</p></aside>
    </section>

