    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1960s</span><span class="rf-text">Liberation theologies are first discussed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, especially within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1970s</span><span class="rf-text">Protestant advocates including C. René Padilla call for integral mission, emphasising evangelism and social responsibility</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1971</span><span class="rf-text">The Peruvian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez">Gustavo Gutiérrez</a> coins the term and writes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-theology-of-liberation">A Theology of Liberation</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980s</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> theology emerges among the Dalit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Mid-1980s</span><span class="rf-text">The Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith admonishes certain forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> liberation theology for their reliance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liberation theology developed within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> in the 1960s as a reaction to the poverty and social injustice of the region, which CEPAL deemed the most unequal in the world. Liberation theologies were first discussed in the Latin American context, especially within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a>.</p><p>Its roots can be traced to precursors such as Catholic Action and to the influence of the Second Vatican Council, which inspired new approaches to addressing systemic poverty and inequality in Latin America. Its theological framework centres on interpreting the Gospel through the lens of the oppressed, and it also drew from broader socialist and anti-imperialist movements.</p><p>Canon law supplied one of its stated foundations: "The Christian faithful are also obliged to promote social justice and, mindful of the precept of the Lord, to assist the poor." More or less at the same time as the first publications of Latin American liberation theology, voices of black liberation theology and Womanist theology were also heard, so that several theologies of liberation arose in the same years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez.jpg" alt="Gustavo Gutiérrez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez">Gustavo Gutiérrez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustavo_gutierrez_(Peruvian_theologian).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mohan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The central idea of the movement is the option for the poor, a phrase popularised by Frei Betto, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez">Gustavo Gutiérrez</a>, Leonardo Boff and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> Juan Luis Segundo and Jon Sobrino. In the form it takes in canon law, it indicates an obligation, on the part of those who would call themselves Christian, first and foremost to care for the poor and vulnerable.</p><p>Liberation theology often engages in socio-economic analysis and emphasises social concern for those marginalised because of their social class, race, ethnicity, gender and similar grounds. Its theoretical foundations draw heavily from Marxist social analysis, particularly its critique of structural inequality and class oppression. It does not adopt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> wholesale, but its use of concepts such as class struggle and its critique of global <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> led to significant controversy within the Church.</p><p>In practice it was a driving force in the rise of grassroots ecclesial communities, in Spanish comunidades eclesiales de base. These communities mobilised marginalised populations to advocate for land reform, labour rights and democratic governance, and often served as spaces for political consciousness-raising, blending religious practices with calls for systemic change.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscar-romero.jpg" alt="Óscar Romero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero">Óscar Romero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monse%C3%B1or_Romero_(colour).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Arzobispado de San Salvador; Congregatio de Causis Sanctorum, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The term "liberation theology" was coined in 1971 by the Peruvian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez">Gustavo Gutiérrez</a>, who wrote one of the movement's defining books, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-theology-of-liberation">A Theology of Liberation</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> also produced Protestant advocates, among them Rubem Alves, José Míguez Bonino and C. René Padilla, who in the 1970s called for integral mission, emphasising evangelism and social responsibility.</p><p>The movement reached its height in the 1970s and 1980s. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, figures such as Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero">Óscar Romero</a> became symbols of resistance against military regimes and human rights abuses. During the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, the archbishop Hélder Câmara called on clergy to engage in the struggle for justice without fear of identification with the revolutionary left, and refused to condemn armed resistance. In an interview with the Italian journalist Oriana Fallaci he said that it was not his choice, "not my road, not my way to apply the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a>", but that he would never say "to use weapons against an oppressor is immoral or anti-Christian".</p><p>In the mid-1980s the Vatican's Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith (CDF) issued an admonition because of the movement's reliance on "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>". Latin American liberation theology also influenced parts of the evangelical movement and Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu.jpg" alt="Desmond Tutu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archbishop-Tutu-medium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Benny Gool, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Peruvian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavo-gutierrez">Gustavo Gutiérrez</a> coined the term and wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-theology-of-liberation">A Theology of Liberation</a>. Other exponents include Leonardo Boff of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, Jon Sobrino of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and Juan Luis Segundo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, and Frei Betto helped to popularise the phrase "preferential option for the poor". Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero">Óscar Romero</a> became a symbol of resistance in El Salvador, and the archbishop Hélder Câmara a voice for engagement in the struggle for justice under military regimes.</p><p>Protestant advocates included Rubem Alves, José Míguez Bonino and C. René Padilla. Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, James Hal Cone was one of the original advocates of black theology, which he described as "a rational study of the being of God in the world in light of the existential situation of an oppressed community, relating the forces of liberation to the essence of the Gospel, which is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a>". The writings of Allan Boesak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Desmond Tutu</a> show how liberationist ideas shaped anti-apartheid activism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</p><p>In Palestinian liberation theology, the Anglican cleric Naim Ateek founded the Sabeel Ecumenical Liberation Theology Center in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and the Catholic priest Rafiq Khoury wrote mostly in Arabic. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>, the priest Des Wilson defended the right of communities failed by the state and the churches to create alternative institutions. Ali Shari'ati in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> adapted liberationist principles to critique Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> within a Shi'a framework.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii.jpg" alt="Pope John Paul II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ADAMELLO_-_PAPA_-_Giovanni_Paolo_II_-_panoramio_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gregorini Demetrio, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Liberation theology generated grassroots ecclesial communities that mobilised marginalised populations and influenced political and social movements across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>. It has been particularly influential in countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Its prominence has waned since its height in the 1970s and 1980s.</p><p>It also faced significant criticism. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> and the CDF, led by Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger, who later became Pope Benedict XVI, criticised it for what they perceived as excessive politicisation and alignment with communist ideologies. Ratzinger stated that "in itself, the expression 'theology of liberation' is a thoroughly valid term", but rejected certain forms of Latin American liberation theology for focusing on institutionalised or systemic sin and for identifying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> hierarchy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> as members of the same privileged class that had long been oppressing Indigenous populations from the arrival of Pizarro onward.</p><p>Some within the Catholic Church questioned its alignment with Marxist ideologies, particularly its use of class analysis and its perceived politicisation of theology. Others argued that its political activism blurred the boundaries between religion and politics, raising concerns about the church's role in revolutionary movements.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-francis.jpg" alt="Pope Francis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pope_Francis_Korea_Haemi_Castle_19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jeon Han, Korea.net, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Theologies of liberation have developed in other parts of the world: black theology in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, Palestinian liberation theology, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalit</a> theology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, Minjung theology in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, liberation theology in Ireland, Deaf liberation theology and a Christian peace movement associated with liberation theology. Dalit theology emerged in the 1980s, two decades after its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> counterpart, shares a number of its themes, and sees hope in the "<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> Manifesto" of Luke 4.</p><p>Liberation theology continues to influence contemporary theological and social movements. In the context of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/globalisation">globalisation</a>, its critique of neoliberal policies and its emphasis on solidarity among oppressed groups remain relevant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-francis">Pope Francis</a>, the first Latin American pope, drew from liberationist ideas, particularly in his advocacy for economic justice and environmental stewardship, as seen in his encyclical Laudato Si'. This development signalled a renewed engagement with the movement's principles within the institutional Church.</p><p>Its legacy also includes its impact on academic discourse, where it has fostered interdisciplinary approaches to theology, sociology and political science. Scholars have analysed its role in decolonising knowledge and creating counter-hegemonic narratives, particularly within the Global South.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Liberation theology is a Christian approach, begun among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> Catholic theologians in the 1960s, that reads the Gospel through the experience of the oppressed and urges an option for the poor. The Vatican criticised its use of Marxist analysis, yet it spread across regions and traditions and still shapes theology and social movements.</em></p>

